  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 252 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Tonight...\n\nQuiet weather will continue through the period, with surface high \npressure in control. Scattered cumulus will dissipate this evening, \nbut there will be some increase in high and perhaps mid level clouds \nby morning.\n\nThe weak lift ahead of an approaching trough that will increase the \ncloudiness overnight will also try to bring some light showers to \nthe northwest portion of the area toward sunrise. The lower \natmosphere should remain dry enough to evaporate any rain that \nforms, so will continue a dry forecast.\n\nLows tonight will generally be in the upper 50s to lower 60s.\n\nWednesday and Wednesday night...\n\nThe initial round of high and mid clouds and potential virga will \nmove across the area early Wednesday. An upper trough and surface \ncold front will move into the area through Wednesday night. Best \nforcing will remain north of the area closer to better upper energy. \n\nAdvection on the west side of the surface high will bring in upper \n60s to around 70 degree dewpoints. With highs in the 80s, decent \ninstability will build. Shear will increase as the upper trough \nmoves in, especially north. \n\nThe combination of these will lead to a severe weather threat mainly \n400 PM to Midnight. Damaging winds and large hail will be the \nprimary threats, but with the higher shear north, isolated tornadoes \nwill be possible in the northern third or so of the forecast area.\n\nWith the best forcing north of the area, will keep PoPs in the \nchance category and used scattered coverage wording. \n\nCoverage will diminish Wednesday night as the front sags south and \ninstability diminishes.\n\nThursday...\n\nThe front will linger in the southern forecast area, so will keep \nsome lower PoPs there. Decent shear/instability looks to remain east \nof the area, so not expecting any severe weather on Thursday. Highs \nwill be in the lower to middle 80s.\n\nFriday and beyond...\n\nAn upper ridge will push its way into central Indiana for much of \nthis period. The result will be warming temperatures, with highs in \nthe middle 80s to lower 90s common by Sunday. Heat indices will \nrise into the lower to middle 90s early next week.\n\nThe ridge should keep the area dry through the day Monday. \nUncertainty on how the upper pattern evolves leads to some low PoPs \nfor Tuesday, but confidence is low.",
